{"date": "2026-11-19", "post_id": 1512728, "title": "The Horsehead and Flame Nebulas", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-19-the-horsehead-and-flame-nebulas/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> Today's <a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/i/ju7bwr?r=B\">image</a> could have been a painting. But what do these colors mean? The brightest star, left of the center, is blue <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-november-20-alnitak-alnilam-mintaka/\">Alnitak</a> (the Girdle): <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alnitak\">a triple star system</a> best known for being the easternmost star in <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stories/quick-reads/discovering-the-universe-through-the-constellation-orion/\">Orion's Belt</a>. The instantly recognizable shape of a dark horse's head at the center is the aptly named <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-september-2-the-horsehead-and-flame-nebulas/\">Horsehead Nebula</a>. It is about five light-years tall and lies approximately 1,500 light-years away. It is made of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-december-10-the-horsehead-nebula/\">dust and gas</a> that obscure the red glow behind it, which is caused by hydrogen gas being ionized by the bright star <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sigma_Orionis\">Sigma Orionis</a> at the top. The orange nebula with dark filaments on the left is the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flame_Nebula\">Flame Nebula</a>. Closer to the Horsehead, the blue <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stories/quick-reads/decoding-nebulae/\">reflection nebula</a> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-november-20-the-horsehead-nebula/\">NGC 2023</a> is actively forming stars. The data for this \"painting\" were collected for almost six hours at the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utah\">Utah</a> <a href=\"https://utahdesertremote.com/\">Desert Remote Observatories</a>, but the equipment was <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1596965349990-6d67ce6b0c1c\">remotely controlled</a> by the astrophotographer from his home, over 2,000 km away in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illinois\">Illinois</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> V-G-R", "credit": "<a href=\"https://www.pop-imagery.com/\">Mark E. Killion</a>", "copyright": "<a href=\"https://www.pop-imagery.com/\">Mark E. Killion</a>", "alt": "Bright stars and colorful nebulas shine on a dark field of stars. A small, dark nebula near the center looks like a horse&#039;s head.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-19-the-horsehead-and-flame-nebulas/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Horsehead_Nebula_Killion.jpg?w=6000&h=4800&fit=clip", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 November 19 - The Horsehead and Flame Nebulas</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-19-the-horsehead-and-flame-nebulas/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Cecilia Chirenti\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Today&#039;s image could have been a painting. But what do these colors mean? The brightest star, left of the center, is blue Alnitak (the Girdle): a triple star system best known for being the easternmost star in Orion&#039;s Belt. The instantly recognizable shape of a dark horse&#039;s head at the center is the aptly named Horsehead Nebula. It is about five light-years tall and lies approximately 1,500 light-years away. It is made of dust and gas that obscure the red glow behind it, which is caused by hydrogen gas being ionized by the bright star Sigma Orionis at the top. The orange nebula with dark filaments on the left is the Flame Nebula. Closer to the Horsehead, the blue reflection nebula NGC 2023 is actively forming stars. The data for this &quot;painting&quot; were collected for almost six hours at the Utah Desert Remote Observatories, but the equipment was remotely controlled by the astrophotographer from his home, over 2,000 km away in Illinois.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: V-G-R\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"the horsehead and flame nebulas\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"The Horsehead and Flame Nebulas\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-19-the-horsehead-and-flame-nebulas/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Horsehead_Nebula_Killion.jpg?w=6000&#038;h=4800&#038;fit=clip\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Horsehead_Nebula_Killion.jpg?w=6000&#038;h=4800&#038;fit=clip\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"Bright stars and colorful nebulas shine on a dark field of stars. A small, dark nebula near the center looks like a horse&#039;s head.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-11-19T00:05:00-05:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-11-19T09:51:25-05:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"The Horsehead and Flame Nebulas\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Horsehead_Nebula_Killion.jpg?w=6000&#038;h=4800&#038;fit=clip\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 November 19<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Horsehead_Nebula_Killion.jpg\" onMouseOver=\"if (document.images) document.imagename1.src='https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Horsehead_Nebula_Killion_annotated_large_font.jpeg';\"\nonMouseOut=\"if (document.images) document.imagename1.src='https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Horsehead_Nebula_Killion.jpg';\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/november/Horsehead_Nebula_Killion.jpg\" name=imagename1 alt=\"Bright stars and colorful nebulas shine on a dark field of stars. A small, dark nebula near the center looks like a horse&#039;s head.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> The Horsehead and Flame Nebulas </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\n<a href=\"https://www.pop-imagery.com/\">Mark E. Killion</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> Today's <a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/i/ju7bwr?r=B\">image</a> could have been a painting. But what do these colors mean? The brightest star, left of the center, is blue <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-november-20-alnitak-alnilam-mintaka/\">Alnitak</a> (the Girdle): <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alnitak\">a triple star system</a> best known for being the easternmost star in <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stories/quick-reads/discovering-the-universe-through-the-constellation-orion/\">Orion's Belt</a>. The instantly recognizable shape of a dark horse's head at the center is the aptly named <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-september-2-the-horsehead-and-flame-nebulas/\">Horsehead Nebula</a>. It is about five light-years tall and lies approximately 1,500 light-years away. It is made of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2025-december-10-the-horsehead-nebula/\">dust and gas</a> that obscure the red glow behind it, which is caused by hydrogen gas being ionized by the bright star <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sigma_Orionis\">Sigma Orionis</a> at the top. The orange nebula with dark filaments on the left is the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flame_Nebula\">Flame Nebula</a>. Closer to the Horsehead, the blue <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stories/quick-reads/decoding-nebulae/\">reflection nebula</a> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2023-november-20-the-horsehead-nebula/\">NGC 2023</a> is actively forming stars. The data for this \"painting\" were collected for almost six hours at the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Utah\">Utah</a> <a href=\"https://utahdesertremote.com/\">Desert Remote Observatories</a>, but the equipment was <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1596965349990-6d67ce6b0c1c\">remotely controlled</a> by the astrophotographer from his home, over 2,000 km away in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illinois\">Illinois</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> V-G-R\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-november-18-an-m-class-solar-flare/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nCecilia Chirenti, Robert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261119/html"}